(version 1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vayu F7 - board-specific design rules
#
# Fab and assembly: JLCPCB, 4-layer FR-4, 1 oz outer, Economic PCBA, both sides.
# Their published limits for this stack-up:
#   track / space   0.09 mm (3.5 mil)      copper to routed edge   0.2 mm
#   drill           0.15 - 6.3 mm          hole position tol.      +/- 0.05 mm
#   soldermask dam  0.10 mm                silkscreen line         0.15 mm
#   PCBA minimum board 10 x 10 mm, 0402 and 0.4 mm lead pitch
# This board is designed to 0.15 mm track / 0.10 mm space, and to vias of
# 0.45-0.70 mm diameter over a 0.20-0.35 mm drill, every one of them carrying at
# least a 0.125 mm annular ring (R3; JLCPCB's stated minimum is 0.13 mm and they
# charge nothing for a 0.20 or 0.25 mm hole while the via is 0.45 mm or wider).
# That sits inside the fab's limits with margin rather than against them: a
# design that needs the fab's best number everywhere is a design that yields badly.
#
# Board Setup carries the global constraints.  What is written here is either an
# exception with a reason, or a constraint the project file cannot express.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GCT's, and now HRO's, USB-C land pattern puts non-plated alignment posts close
# to the connector's own outer pads.  That is the connector manufacturer's
# drawing, not a choice this design gets to make - the posts must line up with
# the pegs moulded into the shell.  The holes are non-plated and the copper
# around them belongs to the same connector, so the only risk is drill wander
# into a pad, and JLCPCB holds hole position to +/-0.05 mm.
(rule "USB-C shell alignment posts"
	(constraint hole_clearance (min 0.15mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('J8') && B.memberOfFootprint('J8')"))

# Battery copper carries the ESC current, and the pack path is wider than the
# electrical minimum on purpose: these are the traces a crash-damaged board is
# most likely to lift, and copper is free.
#
# The number in this rule is 0.25 mm, far below what the pack path is drawn to,
# and that is deliberate.  VBAT is one net with eighteen pads and two jobs.  A
# few millimetres of it carry the whole pack current from the battery pads to
# each converter's VIN pin; the rest feeds two 294 kohm enable dividers, a
# 10 kohm sense divider and the stubs into the bulk capacitors - a quarter of a
# milliamp through the first three and ripple current through the last.  DRC
# sees one net and cannot tell a trunk from a branch, so a rule written for the
# trunk either fails on sixty millimetres of branch or is not written at all.
# 0.25 mm is what this rule is for: catching a gross mistake anywhere on the
# net, well clear of the 0.09 mm the fab can build.
#
# The trunk is checked by a widest-path analysis from the battery entry to each
# VIN pin, which reports the narrowest link on the best path.  That is a question
# about a path; a script can answer it and DRC cannot.  As drawn at R3: 0.50 mm
# at the narrowest to U1, U2 and D1, 0.70-0.80 mm over most of the run.  0.50 mm
# of 1 oz outer copper carries 1.94 A at a 20 C rise, and VBAT now enters on J1
# pin 1 alone - one JST-SH contact rated 1 A - so the connector, not the copper,
# is what limits this board's input.
(rule "battery trace width"
	(constraint track_width (min 0.25mm))
	(condition "A.NetClass == 'Battery'"))

# The switching node of each buck is deliberately short and wide.  Stated as a
# rule so that a later edit that necks it down gets caught by DRC rather than by
# a customer with lines through their video.  Both nodes are 0.45 mm end to end
# as drawn, 6.9 mm long on the 9 V rail and 14.2 mm on the 5 V.
(rule "switch node width"
	(constraint track_width (min 0.45mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == 'SW5V' || A.NetName == 'SW10V' || A.NetName == 'SW3V3'"))

# USB 2.0 full-speed differential pair: 90 ohm nominal on this 4-layer stack-up
# with 0.25 mm traces over the In1 ground plane.
(rule "USB differential pair"
	(constraint track_width (min 0.2mm) (opt 0.25mm))
	(condition "A.NetClass == 'USB'"))

# The three constraints below live here rather than in Board Setup because the
# board editor rewrites the project file from its own in-memory copy on every
# save, and these kept reverting to KiCad's defaults.  Stated as rules they
# survive, and they are the JLCPCB numbers.
(rule "copper to board edge"
	(constraint edge_clearance (min 0.2mm)))
# Scoped to the silkscreen, which is what they were always about.  Without the
# condition they also govern the fabrication layers, where the reference
# designators live - text nobody prints, on a layer that exists for the
# assembler's machine - and that produced 248 warnings about ink that will
# never be laid down.
(rule "silkscreen text height"
	(constraint text_height (min 0.8mm))
	(condition "A.Layer == 'F.Silkscreen' || A.Layer == 'B.Silkscreen' || A.Layer == 'F.SilkS' || A.Layer == 'B.SilkS'"))
(rule "silkscreen line width"
	(constraint text_thickness (min 0.13mm))
	(condition "A.Layer == 'F.Silkscreen' || A.Layer == 'B.Silkscreen' || A.Layer == 'F.SilkS' || A.Layer == 'B.SilkS'"))
